const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const blogJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: publishedDateISO,
  dateModified: publishedDateISO,
  mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: authorName, url: authorUrl },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Future Career School', url: 'https://futurecareerschool.com' },
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[blogJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>MBA career options in India come down to nine lanes, and your institute decides which of them are open on day one.</strong>
    At the top institutes almost every lane is available, from consulting to product to finance. Below that, the
    doors narrow to BFSI, sales, operations, and HR, and the real growth comes from a second job and a skill layer
    you build yourself. Picking the lane before the fee form is how you protect lakhs of rupees, and pairing the
    degree with a high-income skill portfolio is what creates better options and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Consulting, product, and finance pay the most, but at entry they are mostly top-institute doors. IIM Calcutta 2026 averaged Rs 36 LPA; some IIMs averaged far less.</li>
      <li>Sales, relationship, operations, and HR roles hire in the largest numbers and are where most graduates start.</li>
      <li>Regulator roles like RBI Grade B and SEBI Grade A are exam routes, and RBI Grade B does not require an MBA.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick a lane by weekly work, your institute's real recruiter list, and the proof you can show, then plan the second job.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Role lists are everywhere. The harder decision behind them is which door to walk through, and you will find more like it in
    our <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options library</a>. If you are weighing a degree fee against a
    specific lane and want a plan you can show your family,
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    helps you compare lanes, proof, and money before you commit.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer on MBA career options</h2>

  <p>
    People search "career after MBA", "career opportunities after MBA", or "MBA careers" and get the same list of
    ten job titles with salary ranges that disagree with each other. One popular site lists investment banker pay at
    Rs 3-60 LPA. Another lists Rs 6-18.3 LPA for the same title.
  </p>

  <p>
    The list is not the problem. The missing piece is the door: which of those roles will actually take a graduate
    from your institute in the first year, and what happens by year three.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Related guides answer different questions. To choose an elective, read
      <a href="/blog/college-degrees/best-mba-specializations-india/">best MBA specializations in India</a>.
      For the pay curve, read
      <a href="/blog/college-degrees/mba-salary-growth-over-time-india/">MBA salary growth over time</a>.
      For the finance-versus-marketing fork, read
      <a href="/blog/college-degrees/mba-finance-vs-marketing-india/">MBA finance vs marketing</a>.
      Here the question is where the degree can actually take you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lane-map">The nine MBA career lanes, led by growth headroom</h2>

  <p>
    We ordered the lanes by how far income and responsibility can scale, not by prestige. Consulting and product
    lead because pay and scope can compound fastest. Ownership sits last because it has no placement route at all.
  </p>

  <div class="lane-grid">
    {lanes.map((lane) => (
      <div class={`lane-card lane-card--${lane.tone}`}>
        <span class="lane-badge">{lane.badge}</span>
        <h3>{lane.title}</h3>
        <p>{lane.body}</p>
        <p class="lane-row lane-row--fit"><strong>Best for:</strong> {lane.fit}</p>
        <p class="lane-row lane-row--watch"><strong>Watch out:</strong> {lane.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The two-stage filter is real. Getting into a top institute is one competition. Winning a consulting or product
      seat inside it is another. Both stages are hard, so build a backup lane and backup proof before you commit
      to a single dream.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable ownership path:</strong> after a few years in one lane, independent consulting or a small
      advisory practice can scale beyond one salary, because you set the price, choose clients, and can add
      associates. It needs a track record and referrals first, not a fresh degree.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tier-doors">Which MBA doors open at which institute tier</h2>

  <p>
    Use the row that matches your institute, not the one that matches your ambition.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Institute tier</th>
          <th>Recent numbers</th>
          <th>Doors that open at entry</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {tierRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Institute tier"><strong>{row.tier}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Recent numbers">{row.numbers}</td>
            <td data-label="Doors that open at entry">{row.doors}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Figures come from institute and news reports of placement data and are directional. Average, median, and
    highest packages describe different things, so compare like with like.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is also a supply side. AICTE portal data reported by Careers360 shows 4,22,628 approved management seats and
    2,85,367 students enrolled in 2022-23, the latest year we could confirm. That leaves 1,37,261 seats empty, and the
    fill rate has crept up from 60.5% in 2017-18 to 67.5%.
  </p>

  <p>
    CAT 2025 had about 2.95 lakh registrations and 2.58 lakh test takers, and only a small share of them can win a seat at the top institutes.
    So the top of the funnel is tiny and the middle is crowded, and a large number of MBA holders compete for the
    same mid-market lanes.
  </p>

